  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 221 AM CDT Mon Aug 17 2026\n\nCurrently, weak low pressure was located over the area, ahead of\nan approaching cold front dropping south from Canada. Skies were\npartly cloudy and last nights evening convection has mostly\ndissipated except for an isolated shower over northwest ND. We \nremain within a northwest upper level flow. Although modest at\nbest, elevated instability remains over the area with modest \nshear as well. Short term guidance has been overdoing the \novernight convection and continues so. May take a look and see \nhow the northwest convection is going in a bit and if not much \nhas changed, drop from chance to slight chance early this \nmorning. With that said, think there is a chance of showers and \nisolated thunderstorms yet this morning, but probably more like \nmid to late morning as elevated instability and bulk shear aloft\ncontinue to increase. Forecast NAM soundings from Dickinson to \nBismarck and Jamestown indicate 1000-1500 J/kg of SBCape with \n30-40kts of bulk shear and little CIN, late this morning, then \ninstability drop pretty quick this afternoon. If you go down to \nAshley, instability increases to around 2000 J/kg with 35 to 40 \nknots of shear late morning, before also losing instability as \ndrier air moves in this afternoon. SPC has pulled the marginal \nrisk of severe storms (that was east of the forecast area) back \nwest into the far southern James River Valley. Think there is a \nsmall window late morning into the early afternoon for a \nstronger to possibly severe storm here. The main hazards would \nbe quarter sized hail and 60 mph winds.\n\nAfter early this afternoon, the threat for severe storms looks\nto be over for a while. At the surface high pressure builds over\nthe area tonight, but jet energy over the area may provide\nenough forcing for scattered showers and isolated thunderstorms\ntonight into Tuesday, with another wave bringing low chances for\nscattered showers and isolated thunderstorms on Wednesday.\nSevere storms are not expected at this time Tuesday through\nWednesday. \n\nUpper level ridging over the western U.S west amplifies and \npushes east Wednesday through Friday. Northwest flow remains \nover the area so a low threat for a hit or miss thunderstorm \nwill remain, but overall, precipitation chances will remain low \nfor the second half of the work week. \n\nTemperatures remain seasonable through the forecast period. \nHigh temperatures will generally range from the mid 70s to mid \n80s. A little cooler on Tuesday and a little warmer on Thursday.\nForecast lows are expected to range from the mid 40s to mid \n50s. Again, a little cooler on Tuesday, and with some upper 50s \nto lower 60s later in the week.",
